  pay_note:"Apple Pay available on Safari and supported devices · secure card or Apple Pay checkout via Stripe",
  pay_err:"Could not open checkout. Please try again.",
  pro_welcome:"Welcome to Pro! Your key (or your key upgrade) arrives by email within minutes.",
  don_thanks:"Thank you for your support!",
  fwd_eye:"Looking forward", fwd_h:"Forward test: five macro scenarios",
  fwd_d:"Bitcoin's long-run price has tracked a power law with damped cycles. Here are five futures — from euphoric to bleak. Run a live stochastic simulation of any of them, with correlated on-chain metrics, and watch how the risk score would behave.",
  fwd_pricechart:"Projected price (log)", fwd_riskchart:"Projected risk score", fwd_loading:"Computing scenarios…",
  fwd_note:"Illustrative scenarios, not predictions. The power law is fit to history; each scenario adds a macro drift and a damped 4-year cycle. the score's on-chain valuation is coupled to the projected price (just like the risk-price matrix), so the projected risk responds to each price scenario. Not financial advice.",
  algo_eye:"Full transparency", algo_h:"The algorithm, step by step",
  algo_d:"Every component is causal (uses only past data) and mapped to a 0-1 range. No black boxes.",
  algo_sum1:"Mathematical formulation",
  algo_b1:"For each day, a logistic function maps each metric to [0,1]:",
  algo_b2:"Eleven causal signals, grouped by factor:",
  algo_b3:"They are blended with per-factor weights (renormalized over available signals) and smoothed with a short exponential moving average:",
  algo_b4:"Confidence (0-10) rewards the signals agreeing and the risk being extreme. Signals marked * are proxy approximations.",
  comp_eye:"Under the hood", comp_h:"Components & normalization", comp_d:"How each of the eleven signals is measured and mapped to 0–1, and the methodology, live data, and limits behind the score.", comp_sum:"What each signal measures and why",
  comp_c1:"Signal", comp_c2:"Measures", comp_c3:"Source", comp_c4:"Mapping → [0,1]", comp_c5:"Weight", src_price:"price", src_onchain:"on-chain", comp_mayer:"Price vs. 200-day average", comp_logdev_n:"Log-dev",
  comp_logdev:"How far price is from its trend", comp_ath_n:"ATH fraction", comp_ath:"Closeness to the all-time high",
  comp_roc:"90-day price change", comp_mvrv:"Market value vs. realized value", comp_puell:"Miner revenue vs. its yearly average",
  comp_mvrvz:"MVRV in standard deviations (z-score)", comp_sip_n:"Supply in profit", comp_sip:"Share of supply acquired below the current price (cost-basis proxy)", comp_term_n:"Terminal price", comp_term:"Price vs. the terminal-price ceiling (proxy)",
  comp_mctc:"Market cap vs. cumulative security spend (thermocap)", comp_mctcmod:"Mcap/Thermocap as deviation from its trend (interpreted)", comp_rhodl:"Exchange-inflow pressure as a holder-behavior proxy (RHODL needs coin-age data)", comp_fees_n:"Fees", comp_fees:"Transaction fees vs. their yearly average (smoothed)",
  fac_trend:"Price trend", fac_a:"A · Valuation", fac_b:"B · Miners", fac_c:"C · Security-adjusted valuation", fac_d:"D · Holder behavior", fac_e:"E · Network demand", src_proxy:"proxy", src_interp:"interp.",
  comp_b2:"<b>Proxy signals (*):</b> four ITC signals — supply in profit, terminal price, mCTC and RHODL — need coin-age / cost-basis data (Glassnode) the Coin Metrics feed doesn't carry. They are implemented as transparent approximations from available data, at low weight, so the core score stays dominated by the five real signals (MVRV, MVRV-Z, Puell, Mcap/Thermocap and fees).",
  comp_b1:"<b>Key design choice:</b> each transform is anchored to an absolute level, not a rolling percentile. So as Bitcoin matures and each valuation peak comes in lower than the last, the risk peaks compress too — something a percentile re-scaling would erase.",
  met_eye:"Honesty", met_h:"Methodology, live data and limits", met_sum1:"Data sources and live updates",
  met_b1:"History and on-chain metrics come from public Coin Metrics data, embedded when the page is generated. The \u201CRefresh price\u201D button pulls the current Bitcoin price from public sources (CoinGecko, falling back to Coinbase/Kraken), recomputes the price-driven components and holds the on-chain ones at their last known value.",
  met_b2:"The live-recomputed score matches the reference model exactly, so the live point is continuous with the historical line.",
  met_sum2:"Calibration and hindsight bias",
  met_b3:"The logistic centers were fit over the full history, so the thresholds \u201Cknow\u201D where past tops and bottoms were. <b>Live-forward behavior will be more muted than the backtest.</b> Treat the magnitudes as indicative, not a forecast.",
  bs_eye:"Buying strategies", bs_h:"Three ways to buy with the risk metric",
  bs_d:"Three example strategies \u2014 by risk tolerance \u2014 for turning the risk score into an accumulation plan. Buying side only. Pick one below to see its full algorithm and price examples.",
  bs_g1_t:"What the risk metric actually does",
  bs_g1_p1:"The risk score (0.0\u20131.0) is a transparent composite of price behavior, distance from all-time highs and a broader set of on-chain signals (valuation, miners, security-adjusted valuation, holder behavior and demand). Low (near 0) marks periods that have historically offered better value; high (near 1) marks overextension.",
  bs_g1_p2:"<b>Core purpose:</b> it's a buying-timing optimizer for the accumulation phase \u2014 deploy more when value looks better (more Bitcoin per dollar). It is not a price prediction, a short-term trading signal, or a forecast.",
  bs_g2_t:"Accumulation only \u2014 no selling rules",
  bs_g2_p1:"Bitcoin is a one-way road for long-term holders: fixed supply and growing adoption create a strong upward drift over multi-year cycles. For most people the highest-probability plan is to <b>accumulate consistently and never sell</b>.",
  bs_g2_p2:"These three strategies improve only the buying phase \u2014 selling risks missing the next leg up and incurs taxes and friction. The goal is simply to buy cheaper on average than blind DCA.",
  bs_bands:"All three use the <b>same risk bands</b> but differ in aggressiveness and capital needs. They assume a base weekly (or monthly) amount you're comfortable deploying \u2014 e.g. <span class=\"mono\">$100/week</span>. The <b>multiplier</b> scales how much you actually buy in each band.",
